==Latin==

===Etymology===
Related to {{m|la|volō||I fly}} or from {{inh|la|itc-pro|*weksloks}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*weǵʰ-|t=to bring, transport}} (whence {{m|la|vehō}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|vēlōx}}

===Adjective===
{{la-adj|vēlōx|comp=vēlōcior|sup=vēlocissimus|adv=vēlōciter}}

# [[swift]], [[quick]], [[fleet]], [[rapid]], [[speedy]]

====Declension====
{{la-adecl|vēlōx}}
====Usage notes====
According to [https://www.latinitium.com/latin-dictionaries?t=lsn7988,lsn8458,do91 Döderlein], {{m|la|celer}} and {{m|la|citus}} mean "swift, fast, quick"  in terms of ''quick motion'' (in general) with {{m|la|tardus}} as their antonym. More specifically, '''''citus''''' refers to a ''lively'' motion, whereas '''''celer''''' refers to an ''eager'' or ''impetuous'' motion.

On the other hand, ''vēlōx'' and {{m|la|pernīx}} as "quick" denote a level of athletic nimbleness in terms of ''bodily activity'', with {{m|la|lentus}} as their antonym. More specifically, '''''pernīx''''' involves a level of ''dexterity'' and quickness in an eclectic range of actions (such as climbing, hurdling, jumping, vaulting, etc.); whereas '''''vēlōx''''' is especially used for running, swimming and flying (moving in a ''direction'') 

Thirdly, {{m|la|properus}} and {{m|la|festīnus}} as "qucik" refer specifically to one's ''speed'' in terms of the shortest time to reach a desitnation, with {{m|la|sēgnis}} as their antonym. More specifically, '''''festīnus''''' intimates a certain level of ''impatience'', whereas '''''properus''''' simply indicates a ''haste'' from ''energy'' simply.

